From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 16:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] gamesave: ask the host at a save conflict; file saves play
 locally offline

At a sync, a container that changed here and in the cloud since the last
sync (dirty, and the listing's clientFileTime is not the one last synced)
failed the whole sync with E_GS_CONTAINER_NOT_IN_SYNC: the session then
played on local saves and the conflict stayed until something forced a side
(XODUS_SAVE_SYNC=download/upload). Nothing asked the player.

cloud_sync now asks once per sync through SaveTransport::Conflict, which
sends the host a SaveConflictRequest (Scid, Container, DisplayName,
LocalTime, CloudTime and CloudDevice, the listing's deviceName when it has
one) over the keyed bridge as message type 0x5001, and waits for the
player, as a game waits on the system's dialog. The reply's
<Choice>local</Choice> uploads the local containers whole over the cloud's
(force_upload); <Choice>cloud</Choice> downloads the cloud's, the local tree
kept as a .recovery- copy as for any download; anything else, or no host,
keeps both as before: local saves this session, the dirty index kept, the
question again at the next connected session.

The file-save provider (XGameSaveFiles) failed outright when the cloud
could not be reached or locked. Like the container provider (cloud_disable)
it now plays on local saves for the session, its changes left dirty for the
next connected one.

tests/Nexus/gamesave-sync.cpp covers the question's fields, no host, an
unanswered question, the local choice (an upload attempted, the local data
still unsynced when it fails) and the cloud choice (downloaded and synced),
for both providers; its required check count rises to 315.

Playport's lines in this patch are offered under LGPL-2.1-or-later.

Class: feature
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-30-xbox-ground-truth-nexus.md
Offered-upstream: no
